WEBVTT

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:12.455
Now watch your yarn. It takes at least three times the width of the working yarn to knit one row.

00:00:12.655 --> 00:00:33.274
I noticed I was running low, so I unthreaded the yarn and laid it across the needle bed. It didn't quite measure three times, so I'm going to cut the yarn and start a new ball.

00:00:33.474 --> 00:00:45.875
To work in that first yarn tail, I pulled out a few needles and ran the tail over and under those needles.

00:00:46.075 --> 00:01:04.176
I threaded up the new ball of yarn, leave a tail of the new ball, and knit across the row, securing the first tail. You can safely cut that first tail now.

00:01:04.376 --> 00:01:16.602
Before you knit back, wrap the new tail over and under the opposite needles at the beginning of that row.

00:01:16.802 --> 00:01:27.458
Knit across and secure that second tail. And at any time, it's safe to cut that second tail.

00:01:27.658 --> 00:01:32.670
Continue knitting, watching your row counter.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:45.060
This is a neat and tidy way to secure yarn tails when knitting stripes or adding a new ball of yarn without having to work them in when you're done knitting.

